# Tests from nvm's own suite (tests/reference/nvm-v0.40.8/test) that are not
# run as parity cases, as `<reason>: <path under test/>`. A path ending in `/`
# covers a whole directory. Reasons:
#
#   installer      tests nvm's install.sh, which nvmimic does not ship
#   fixture        an executable file the tests use, not a test itself
#   docker         only meaningful inside nvm's Ubuntu 16.04 test image
#   mock-internal  redefines one of nvm's internal functions as a shell
#                  function and expects nvm to call it. nvmimic notices that
#                  under bash, sh and zsh and runs nvm's own code for the
#                  call. dash cannot show what a function contains, so these
#                  tests run under every shell but dash
#   mock-tool      redefines an outside program (clang, wget) as a shell
#                  function or alias and expects nvm to run that instead.
#                  Not under dash, like mock-internal
#   replaces-nvm   redefines the function `nvm` itself and then calls one of
#                  nvm's helpers. nvmimic's helpers reach its program through
#                  `nvm`, so the helper ends up in the test's function
#   copied-lines   compares the shell's own error lines from a function that
#                  nvmimic's nvm.sh copies from nvm. The copy sits on other
#                  line numbers than in nvm's nvm.sh
#   network        needs the real internet or Docker
#   real-node      installs real Node releases from nodejs.org (0.8 to 9.x and
#                  io.js, some built from source), which the offline test
#                  mirror does not hold. `cargo xtask parity --network` runs
#                  these and nothing else
#
#   real-node-mock both real-node and mock-internal: in the network pass
#                  only, and not under dash
#   real-node-hook installs real Node and names a shell function as
#                  NVM_INSTALL_THIRD_PARTY_HOOK: in the network pass only,
#                  and not under dash
#   real-node-npm  installs real Node and then many packages from the live npm
#                  registry at once. npm prints them in whatever order they
#                  arrive, so in the network pass only the exit status and
#                  the files are compared
#
# A line that names one test wins over a line that names its directory.
